{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-12124" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-2,-1),(1,3),(2,-3).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(-3,1)$\n- $(-3,2)$\n- $(1,-2)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/state.json" "file_sha256" "0642b06331e49f39ba3507b0128498f7b8548369900c259748e072c00b6326f6"} {"question" "q-12124" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of ${90}^{∘}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,-2),(-3,1),(3,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/state.json" "file_sha256" "0642b06331e49f39ba3507b0128498f7b8548369900c259748e072c00b6326f6"} {"question" "q-12124" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d5412300abaf3d9cfe40c7fe60724db3bf41b03e257c8e1c9aa4b8a19eb15cd3"} {"question" "q-12124" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/state.json" "file_sha256" "0642b06331e49f39ba3507b0128498f7b8548369900c259748e072c00b6326f6"} {"question" "q-12124" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/state.json" "file_sha256" "0642b06331e49f39ba3507b0128498f7b8548369900c259748e072c00b6326f6"} {"question" "q-12124" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/state.json" "file_sha256" "0642b06331e49f39ba3507b0128498f7b8548369900c259748e072c00b6326f6"} {"question" "q-81829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/assets/df7a1eb6aaf75cc611826af00ce066ed05440bb048ad79d8c0532007b9a89c3a.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/state.json" "file_sha256" "0642b06331e49f39ba3507b0128498f7b8548369900c259748e072c00b6326f6"} {"question" "q-81829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/assets/77936c4115b37d79ae301a93b218ecd23114ae4ad42d8298b8f058c1f50e82a2.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians **clockwise** about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ **clockwise** by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/assets/e941207a92a2b688428c55be3bc17856ea47fa2e11db8e3dc571b88ebbe8e3b1.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/state.json" "file_sha256" "0642b06331e49f39ba3507b0128498f7b8548369900c259748e072c00b6326f6"} {"question" "q-81829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d5412300abaf3d9cfe40c7fe60724db3bf41b03e257c8e1c9aa4b8a19eb15cd3"} {"question" "q-81829" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/state.json" "file_sha256" "0642b06331e49f39ba3507b0128498f7b8548369900c259748e072c00b6326f6"} {"question" "q-81829" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Cours